.TH CF-TUNNELS 1 "September 2026" "cf-cli v1.1.2" "Cloudflare CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
cf-tunnels.1 \- Manage Cloudflare Zero Trust Tunnels and cloudflared tokens
.PP
Manage Cloudflare Tunnels (\fBcloudflared\fR) to securely expose origin servers, private network services, and local development environments without opening public inbound ports.
.PP
.PP
.SH REQUIRED PERMISSIONS
.PP
.PP
.RS 2
.TP
Read
\fIScope Level:\fR Account | \fIMinimum API Token Permission:\fR \fBCloudflare Tunnel:Read\fR
.TP
Write
\fIScope Level:\fR Account | \fIMinimum API Token Permission:\fR \fBCloudflare Tunnel:Edit\fR
.RE
.PP
.PP
.PP
.SH SUBCOMMANDS OVERVIEW
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcf tunnels list\fR (#cf-tunnels-list) — List all tunnels in your account
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcf tunnels get\fR (#cf-tunnels-get) — Get tunnel status and connection health
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcf tunnels create\fR (#cf-tunnels-create) — Provision a new tunnel
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcf tunnels delete\fR (#cf-tunnels-delete) — Remove an inactive tunnel
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcf tunnels token\fR (#cf-tunnels-token) — Retrieve the base64 authentication token for \fBcloudflared\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcf tunnels config\fR (#cf-tunnels-config) — Manage remotely managed ingress routing rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcf tunnels connections\fR (#cf-tunnels-connections) — View active connector edge links
.PP
.PP
.SS "\fBcf tunnels list\fR"
.PP
Lists all tunnels and their status (\fBhealthy\fR, \fBdown\fR, \fBinactive\fR).
.PP
.PP
\fBSyntax\fR
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
cf tunnels list [--is-deleted]
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
.SS "\fBcf tunnels create\fR"
.PP
Provisions a new remotely managed Cloudflare Tunnel.
.PP
.PP
\fBSyntax\fR
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
cf tunnels create --name <tunnel-name>
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
\fBExample\fR
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
cf tunnels create --name internal-k8s-cluster
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
.SS "\fBcf tunnels token\fR"
.PP
Retrieves the secret authentication token used to run the \fBcloudflared\fR daemon in Docker, Kubernetes, or systemd.
.PP
.PP
\fBSyntax\fR
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
cf tunnels token --id <tunnel-id>
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
\fBExample\fR
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
# Run tunnel immediately using Docker
# This command already prints the bare token on stdout; no format flag is needed
TOKEN=$(cf tunnels token --id 7b29a1b0-2345-4b3e-9087-123456abcdef)
docker run -d cloudflare/cloudflared:latest tunnel --no-autoupdate run --token "$TOKEN"
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
.SS "\fBcf tunnels config\fR"
.PP
Manage remotely managed tunnel configurations and ingress rules without editing local YAML files.
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
# View active ingress rules
cf tunnels config get --id <tunnel-id>

# Apply ingress configuration from a JSON file
cf tunnels config update --id <tunnel-id> --file ingress.json
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
\fIExample \fBingress.json\fR:\fR
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
{
  "config": {
    "ingress": [
      {
        "hostname": "grafana.internal.example.com",
        "service": "http://localhost:3000"
      },
      {
        "service": "http_status:404"
      }
    ]
  }
}
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
.SS "\fBcf tunnels connections\fR"
.PP
Inspect active connections between your \fBcloudflared\fR instance and Cloudflare edge data centers.
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
cf tunnels connections --id <tunnel-id>
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
.SS "\fBcf tunnels delete\fR"
.PP
Deletes a tunnel and cleans up its credentials.
.PP
.PP
\fBSyntax\fR
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
cf tunnels delete --id <tunnel-id> [--yes]
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
.PP
\fBExample\fR
.PP
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
cf tunnels delete --id 7b29a1b0-2345-4b3e-9087-123456abcdef --yes
.ft
.fi
.PP
.PP
